\section{Introduction}

The [[-L]] option of [[notangle]] makes compilers report errors in
terms of the [[.nw]] source instead of the tangled output.  It comes
with a documented guarantee: text appears in the same \emph{column} in
input and output, so that column numbers stay accurate too.  To honor
that guarantee, [[nt]] must not indent expanded chunks
(see the chunk \emph{print a string} in [[src/c/modules.nw]]), so all
indentation from chunk uses is lost.  For languages like Python, where
indentation is syntax, [[-L]] therefore produces broken programs.
This is not a bug to fix in [[notangle]]---the column guarantee is
useful for the languages [[-L]] was designed for---so this document
takes the approach suggested in issue~41 of the noweb repository:
leave [[-L]] alone and provide two small companion tools.

\begin{description}
\item[linemark] A noweb filter that inserts a formal comment, for
  example [[# 42 "foo.nw"]], wherever the tangled output jumps to a
  different place in the [[.nw]] source.  We then tangle
  \emph{without} [[-L]], so indentation survives.  Comment-only lines
  are exempt from Python's indentation rules, so the inserted lines
  are harmless.
\item[nolinemap] A companion program that reads the output of any
  tool that refers to tangled files---a compiler, an interpreter, a
  linter, an editor's quickfix stream---finds the file/line
  references, and rewrites them to point into the [[.nw]] source,
  using the comments that [[linemark]] inserted.  It also understands
  the [[#line]] directives produced by [[notangle -L]] itself, so it
  can serve languages with real [[#line]] support, too.  Where the
  message carries a column number, [[nolinemap]] also translates the
  column.  It is also installed under the alias [[noerr]], after the
  older program it supersedes.
\end{description}

A typical session looks like this:

\begin{verbatim}
notangle -filter linemark example.nw > example.py
nolinemap python3 example.py
\end{verbatim}

The first command tangles with location comments; the second runs the
program and rewrites any traceback so that it points into
[[example.nw]].  [[nolinemap]] exits with the wrapped command's exit
status, so both commands can go into a Makefile unchanged.

\subsection{Naming}

The names went through some deliberation, recorded here so the
trade-offs need not be rediscovered.  The guiding observation is that
the two tools do different things to the same information: the filter
\emph{marks} lines, the companion \emph{maps} them back.  Names
should not cross those roles.

\begin{description}
\item[nolinemap + noerr] The first draft.  \emph{noerr} is short,
  purpose-evocative, in the [[no]]* family, and honors
  Sven~K\"onig's original tool.  But it undersells the program: the
  translation is useful to anything that consumes locations in
  tangled files---linters, editor quickfix parsing, vim
  plugins---not just error messages, and \emph{noerr} can even be
  misread as \emph{suppressing} errors.  Worse, naming the
  \emph{filter} \emph{nolinemap} crossed the roles: the filter does
  not map anything.
\item[nolinemap + noremap] Rejected: \emph{noremap} is one of the
  most-typed vim commands in existence, hopeless to search for, and
  the [[no]]-pun reads as a negation---\emph{don't remap}---the
  opposite of what the tool does.
\item[nolinemark + nolinemap] Semantically right, and pretty as a
  pair, but the names differ in one letter: tab completion never
  disambiguates, and a typo swaps the tools.  That slip fails
  \emph{silently}: the mapper, run as a [[-filter]], passes the
  pipeline markup through mostly unchanged, so the tangle succeeds
  with no markers in it.
\item[linemark + nolinemap] Chosen.  Filters in the core tree are
  unprefixed ([[sharpline]], [[emptydefn]]); the [[no]]* names are
  the commands people type.  \emph{linemark} says what the filter
  does and sits next to its closest relative, [[sharpline]];
  \emph{nolinemap} names the mechanism---mapping lines---which is
  honest about serving more than error messages.  \emph{noerr}
  survives as an alias, for fingers and Makefiles that remember it.
\end{description}


\section{The marker contract}

Both tools agree on one \emph{marker format}, by default

\begin{verbatim}
# %L "%F"
\end{verbatim}

where [[%L]] expands to a line number and [[%F]] to a file name (and
[[%%]] to a literal percent sign).  These are the same escapes
[[notangle -L]] uses, minus [[%N]]: a marker is always exactly one
line, so no explicit newline is ever needed.  A marker asserts:
\emph{the next line of this file came from line [[%L]] of file
[[%F]]}.

The filter inserts a marker at every \emph{discontinuity}: near the
start of each code chunk's definition, and after every line that uses
another chunk.  Everything between two consecutive markers is a run
of lines copied verbatim from consecutive lines of the [[.nw]]
file---so [[nolinemap]] can map any tangled line~$T$ by finding the last
marker at some line $M \le T$ pointing at source line~$S$, and
computing $S + (T - M - 1)$.  This contract is what makes the two
programs correct together; everything else is bookkeeping.

\enquote{Near} the start, not \emph{at} it: the marker for a chunk's
first definition follows the chunk's \emph{first line}, announcing
its second.  A marker placed before the first line rides into every
splice of the chunk, and a chunk used in the \emph{middle} of a
line---[[if [ <<test>> ]; then]]---would then have the marker land
inside that line, where the comment leader swallows the rest and the
forced line break makes the tangle differ from the program notangle
builds.  Placed after the first line, the marker always falls on a
line boundary of the tangled program: the splice's first line stays
whole, anchored to the use line by plain counting, and the chunk's
remaining lines are anchored exactly by the marker.  The price is
deliberate: an error on the first line of a used chunk is reported at
the line that uses the chunk rather than at its definition---still
the right file, and the definition is one jump away.

A related guard applies at every marker: a line ending in a
backslash announces, in most of the languages this filter serves,
that the next physical line continues it, and a comment dropped
between the two would sever them.  So a pending marker is never
flushed after a backslash-terminated line; it waits for the next
safe boundary, and the lines it skipped anchor to the last marker by
plain counting.  The count is off by the length of the detour when a
splice intervenes---the honest cost of refusing to break the
program.  Backslashes are the one continuation form a line-based
filter can see; a chunk whose first line opens a multi-line string
remains beyond it (see BUGS in the manual page).

Consumers that post-process the tangled text can do better than
either compromise: with [[-e]] (\emph{eager}), every chunk gets its
marker before its first line, exactly as earlier versions of this
filter placed them, and the backslash guard stands down too---every
discontinuity is marked, no matter where the marker lands.  A
mid-line splice then carries the marker inside the broken line, and
a marker after a continuation line severs it; but a consumer that
recognizes and repairs both---vim-noweb's shadow buffers relocate
the former and drop the latter, keeping their announcements for the
map---recovers both the exact program and an exact map.

The line numbers themselves come from the noweb pipeline
representation.  [[markup]] emits one [[@nl]] for every newline of
the source file---documentation, code, and the [[<<name>>=]] line
alike---so a filter can recover the current source line by counting.
Filters earlier in the pipeline may also inject [[@line n]] (meaning:
the next line came from source line $n$) and [[@file f]]; we honor
both, exactly like [[nt]] does, so [[linemark]] composes with
filters like [[sharpline]].


\section{The filter: linemark}

[[linemark]] is a shell script wrapping an awk program, like most
noweb filters.  It reads the pipeline representation on standard
input and writes it, with markers inserted, on standard output.

<<linemark>>=
#!/bin/sh
#
# linemark [-L fmt] [-c leader] -- insert source-location comments
# into code chunks, so that the output of notangle (without -L!) can
# be mapped back to the .nw source by nolinemap.  See nolinemap.nw.
#
# sample usage:
#   notangle -filter linemark foo.nw > foo.py

<<the [[usage]] function>>
<<parse the linemark command line>>
<<choose an awk implementation>>
LINEMARK_FMT="$fmt" LINEMARK_EAGER="$eager" "$AWK" '
BEGIN { fmt = ENVIRON["LINEMARK_FMT"]; lineno = 1
        eager = ENVIRON["LINEMARK_EAGER"] + 0 }
<<function [[marker]] expands [[fmt]] and emits the comment>>
<<rules: [[@file]] and [[@line]] set the source location>>
<<rules: [[@begin code]] and [[@end code]] delimit chunks>>
<<rules: [[@defn]] and [[@use]] arm a marker>>
<<rules: [[@nl]] counts lines and schedules pending markers>>
<<rules: [[@text]] discharges a pending marker>>
{ print }
' "$@"
@ %def linemark

\subsection{The command line}

The full marker format is set with [[-L]], named after the
[[notangle]] option it replaces.  Often the only thing that varies
between languages is the comment leader, so [[-c]] provides a
shortcut: [[-c //]] yields the format [[// %L "%F"]].  When both are
given, [[-L]] wins, since it is the more explicit of the two.  Both
options accept the argument either as a separate word or glued to the
option, the latter for symmetry with [[notangle -L'...']].  [[-h]]
prints a short help text; the same text doubles as the hint appended
to error messages about bad options.  The here-document keeps the
text in one place, and the quoted delimiter keeps the shell from
expanding anything in it.

<<the [[usage]] function>>=
usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
usage: linemark [-L fmt] [-c leader] [-e]

noweb filter: insert source-location comments into code chunks, so
that output of tools running on the tangled files can be mapped back
to the .nw sources by nolinemap(1).  Unlike notangle -L, indentation
is preserved, so it suits languages like Python.

  -L fmt      marker format; %L = line, %F = file, %% = percent
              (default: # %L "%F")
  -c leader   comment leader for the default format (default: #)
  -e, --eager eager markers: mark the first line of every chunk too,
              even where that lands a marker inside a spliced line;
              for consumers that post-process the output
  -h, --help  print this help and exit

sample usage:
  notangle -filter linemark foo.nw > foo.py
  nolinemap python3 foo.py
EOF
}
@ %def usage

<<parse the linemark command line>>=
fmt=
leader='#'
eager=
while [ $# -gt 0 ]
do
  case "$1" in
    -L)   [ $# -ge 2 ] ||
            { echo "linemark: option -L needs an argument" 1>&2; exit 1; }
          fmt="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    -L?*) fmt="${1#-L}"; shift ;;
    -c)   [ $# -ge 2 ] ||
            { echo "linemark: option -c needs an argument" 1>&2; exit 1; }
          leader="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    -c?*) leader="${1#-c}"; shift ;;
    -e|--eager) eager=1; shift ;;
    -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;;
    --)   shift; break ;;
    -*)   echo "linemark: unknown option $1" 1>&2; usage 1>&2; exit 1 ;;
    *)    break ;;
  esac
done
[ -n "$fmt" ] || fmt="$leader"' %L "%F"'
@

The format is handed to awk through the environment rather than with
[[awk -v]]: POSIX awk applies C escape processing to [[-v]] values,
which would corrupt any format containing a backslash.  [[ENVIRON]]
delivers the string untouched.

The [[awkname]] script that rewrites [[nawk]] to the locally
configured awk does not visit [[contrib]], so the wrapper picks its
own implementation: it honors [[$AWK]] if set, prefers [[nawk]], and
falls back to plain [[awk]].

<<choose an awk implementation>>=
AWK="${AWK-nawk}"
command -v "$AWK" >/dev/null 2>&1 || AWK=awk
@

\subsection{Expanding the format}

[[marker]] expands the format with the current location and emits it
as an [[@text]] line followed by [[@nl]].  Both lines are printed
directly, bypassing the line-counting rule below, because they do not
correspond to any line of the source file.  Unknown escapes are
passed through unchanged, so that a format meant for a future version
degrades visibly instead of silently.

<<function [[marker]] expands [[fmt]] and emits the comment>>=
function marker(    out, i, c) {
  out = ""
  for (i = 1; i <= length(fmt); i++) {
    c = substr(fmt, i, 1)
    if (c == "%" && i < length(fmt)) {
      i++; c = substr(fmt, i, 1)
      if (c == "L")      out = out lineno
      else if (c == "F") out = out file
      else if (c == "%") out = out "%"
      else               out = out "%" c
    } else out = out c
  }
  print "@text " out
  print "@nl"
  pending = 0
}
@ %def marker

\subsection{Tracking the source location}

The counter [[lineno]] always holds the number of the source line
currently being read; it is advanced by that line's terminating
[[@nl]].  [[@file]] restarts the count for a new file.  [[@line n]]
declares that the \emph{next} line is line $n$; because our counter
is incremented only when the following [[@nl]] arrives, storing $n$
directly gives that next line the right number.  ([[nt]] uses the
same convention but increments eagerly, which is why \emph{it} has to
decrement after [[@line]]; see [[src/c/notangle.nw]].)

<<rules: [[@file]] and [[@line]] set the source location>>=
/^@file /       { file = substr($0, 7); lineno = 1; print; next }
/^@line /       { lineno = $2 + 0; print; next }
@

\subsection{Inserting markers}

Markers are inserted \emph{lazily}.  Rather than emitting a marker
the moment a chunk begins, we only note that one is [[pending]] and
emit it just before the next piece of chunk content.  Laziness buys
two properties that eager insertion lacks: a chunk that has no
content produces no output at all (an empty chunk stays empty), and a
chunk whose last line uses another chunk does not end with a dangling
marker that points past its own end.

Two events arm a marker.  The first is the start of a chunk
definition, where placement depends on whether the definition is the
chunk's first or a continuation of an already-seen name.  A
continuation always begins on a fresh line of the tangled output (it
follows the previous definition's final newline), so its marker can
safely precede its first line: [[@defn]] raises [[afterdefn]], and
the [[@nl]] terminating the [[<<name>>=]] line converts it into
[[pending]]---never gluing a marker onto the definition line itself.
A \emph{first} definition is the one that gets spliced at use sites,
so its marker must follow its first line (see the placement argument
above): [[@defn]] raises [[firstdef]] instead, the definition line's
[[@nl]] promotes it, and the [[@nl]] ending the first \emph{content}
line arms [[pending]]---by then [[lineno]] has advanced to the second
content line, which is exactly what the marker should announce.  A
first definition of a single line arms a marker that no content ever
discharges, and [[@end code]] throws it away: such a chunk needs no
marker at all, since its whole splice anchors to the use site.
Under [[-e]] every definition takes the continuation path, restoring
the eager placement.  The second arming event is a chunk use: once
the line containing the [[@use]] ends, the tangled output will have
detoured through the used chunk, so the following content needs
re-anchoring.  A [[@use]] also discharges any marker already
pending, since a use is chunk content in its own right.  Flushes
everywhere are gated on [[contline]], the backslash guard described
above, unless [[-e]] asked for every marker; and a [[@use]] clears
[[bs]]: whatever the spliced chunk ends with, it is not a backslash
we have seen.

<<rules: [[@begin code]] and [[@end code]] delimit chunks>>=
/^@begin code / { code = 1; bs = 0; contline = 0; print; next }
/^@end code /   { code = 0; afterdefn = 0; sawuse = 0; firstdef = 0
                  pending = 0; print; next }
@

<<rules: [[@defn]] and [[@use]] arm a marker>>=
/^@defn /       { name = substr($0, 7)
                  if (eager || (name in seen)) afterdefn = 1
                  else { seen[name] = 1; firstdef = 1 }
                  print; next }
/^@use /        { if (code && pending && (eager || !contline)) marker()
                  if (code) { sawuse = 1; bs = 0 }
                  print; next }
@

The newline rule does three jobs, in an order that matters.  A
pending marker is flushed \emph{before} the [[@nl]] is printed, so
that a blank line at the start of a chunk body still ends up below
the marker that describes it.  Then the line counter advances.  Only
then is a new marker armed, so that the marker's eventual line number
is that of the line \emph{after} the definition or use line.  Both
[[@nl]] and [[@index nl]] count, because [[nt]] counts both.  Lines
with several uses arm [[sawuse]] several times but the terminating
[[@nl]] converts it just once, so such a line gets exactly one
marker.  [[firstdef]] steps through its two states here: the
definition line's [[@nl]] promotes it from~1 to~2, and the next
[[@nl]]---the one ending the chunk's first content line---arms
[[pending]] like the other events do.  The arming branch runs before
the promotion and clears [[firstdef]], so a first line that also
contains a use arms exactly one marker.

<<rules: [[@nl]] counts lines and schedules pending markers>>=
/^(@nl|@index nl)$/ {
  if (code && pending && (eager || !contline)) marker()
  print
  lineno++
  if (code && (afterdefn || sawuse || firstdef == 2)) {
    pending = 1; afterdefn = 0; sawuse = 0; firstdef = 0
  }
  if (code && firstdef == 1) firstdef = 2
  if (code) { contline = bs; bs = 0 }
  next
}
@

Ordinary text discharges a pending marker and passes through, and it
is where the backslash guard learns what it knows: each [[@text]]
records whether it ends in a backslash, the last one on a line
winning, which the [[@nl]] rule then freezes into [[contline]].
Text in documentation chunks is never preceded by a pending marker
because [[pending]] is only ever set while [[code]] is~1, but the
guard keeps the invariant local and obvious.

<<rules: [[@text]] discharges a pending marker>>=
/^@text /       { if (code && pending && (eager || !contline)) marker()
                  if (code) bs = ($0 ~ /\\$/)
                  print; next }
@


\section{The mapper: nolinemap}

[[nolinemap]] undoes at reporting time what tangling did at build
time.  It is written in Python~3 (using only the standard library):
unlike the filter, it juggles several regular expressions with
captures, caches parsed files, and does column arithmetic, all of
which are painful in awk.  Python is only needed when tool output is
being translated, never to tangle or weave, so the dependency is
soft.

The idea comes from an old Perl program by Sven~K\"onig called
\emph{noerr}, which translated [[notangle -L]] output for languages
such as Java.  This program supersedes it---it understands both
comment markers and [[#line]] directives, recognizes Python
tracebacks in addition to \emph{file:line} messages, translates
columns, and can run the failing command itself---and it is installed
under the alias [[noerr]] as well, in its memory.

<<nolinemap>>=
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Translate messages that point into files tangled by noweb so
that they point into the .nw sources instead.

Usage: nolinemap [-L fmt] [-c leader] [--] [command arg ...]

With a command, run it, translate its output, and exit with its exit
status.  Without one, translate standard input.  See nolinemap.nw.
"""

<<imports>>

<<constants>>

<<functions>>

<<classes>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
@ %def nolinemap

<<imports>>=
import argparse
import bisect
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
@

\subsection{Recognizing markers}

The marker format is compiled into a regular expression: literal
characters are escaped, [[%L]] becomes a capture of digits, [[%F]] a
capture of anything, and [[%%]] a literal percent sign.  The
expression tolerates leading blanks because [[nt]] prefixes every
line of an expanded chunk---markers included---with the indentation
of the use site.  Trailing blanks are tolerated for robustness.  A
format without [[%L]] cannot express a line number and is rejected
outright; a format without [[%F]] is allowed and simply yields
markers that name no file, in which case [[noerr]] keeps the tangled
file's name and translates only line numbers.

<<functions>>=
def fmt_to_regex(fmt):
    """Compile a marker format such as '# %L "%F"' to a regex."""
    parts = [r"^[ \t]*"]
    has_line = False
    i = 0
    while i < len(fmt):
        c = fmt[i]
        if c == "%" and i + 1 < len(fmt):
            escape = fmt[i + 1]
            if escape == "L":
                parts.append(r"(?P<line>\d+)")
                has_line = True
                i += 2
                continue
            if escape == "F":
                parts.append(r"(?P<file>.*?)")
                i += 2
                continue
            if escape == "%":
                parts.append(re.escape("%"))
                i += 2
                continue
        parts.append(re.escape(c))
        i += 1
    parts.append(r"[ \t\r]*$")
    if not has_line:
        raise SystemExit("nolinemap: marker format lacks %L: " + fmt)
    return re.compile("".join(parts))
@ %def fmt_to_regex

Whatever format the user gives, two built-in patterns are always
tried as well: the C preprocessor's [[#line n "file"]] and its short
form [[# n "file"]].  These are what [[notangle -L]] emits by
default, so [[nolinemap]] works out of the box for [[-L]]-tangled
languages, with no filter involved.

<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_FMT = '# %L "%F"'
BUILTIN_MARKERS = [
    re.compile(r'^[ \t]*#[ \t]*line[ \t]+(?P<line>\d+)[ \t]+"(?P<file>[^"]*)"'),
    re.compile(r'^[ \t]*#[ \t]+(?P<line>\d+)[ \t]+"(?P<file>[^"]*)"'),
]
@ %def DEFAULT_FMT BUILTIN_MARKERS

\subsection{Mapping a tangled file}

A [[SourceMap]] is the parsed form of one tangled file: its lines
(kept for column translation) and its markers, each recorded as the
tangled line it sits on plus the source line and file it points at.
The marker lists are kept sorted by construction---we scan the file
top to bottom---so lookup can bisect.

<<classes>>=
class SourceMap:
    """The location markers found in one tangled file."""

    def __init__(self, path, lines, markers):
        self.dir = os.path.dirname(path)
        self.lines = lines
        self.points = [m[0] for m in markers]
        self.targets = [(m[1], m[2]) for m in markers]

    <<sourcemap methods>>
@ %def SourceMap

Resolution implements the contract from the start of this document:
the last marker at or above the queried line anchors a run of
verbatim lines, so the source line is the marker's target plus the
distance from the marker.  A query \emph{on} a marker line itself
maps to the marker's target: the marker is not a source line, and its
target is the nearest sensible answer.  A query above the first
marker has no anchor and returns [[None]], which the caller treats as
untranslatable.

<<sourcemap methods>>=
def resolve(self, lineno):
    """Map tangled line `lineno` to (source file, source line).

    The source file is None when the markers name no file.
    Returns None when `lineno` lies before the first marker.
    """
    i = bisect.bisect_right(self.points, lineno) - 1
    if i < 0:
        return None
    marker_line = self.points[i]
    src_line, src_file = self.targets[i]
    if lineno == marker_line:
        return (src_file, src_line)
    return (src_file, src_line + (lineno - marker_line - 1))
@ %def resolve

For column translation we also need the raw text of a tangled line.

<<sourcemap methods>>=
def line_text(self, lineno):
    """Return the text of tangled line `lineno`, or None."""
    if 1 <= lineno <= len(self.lines):
        return self.lines[lineno - 1]
    return None
@ %def line_text

\subsection{The translator}

The [[Translator]] holds the compiled marker patterns and two caches:
parsed tangled files and the line lists of [[.nw]] sources.  Caching
matters because a single traceback mentions the same file many times,
and [[noerr]] may sit in a long-running pipe.

<<classes>>=
class Translator:
    """Rewrite file/line references using cached source maps."""

    def __init__(self, marker_regexes):
        self.marker_regexes = marker_regexes
        self.maps = {}
        self.nw_cache = {}

    <<translator methods>>
@ %def Translator

A file mentioned in an error message earns a [[SourceMap]] only if it
exists, is readable, and contains at least one marker.  Everything
else maps to [[None]], which makes references to it pass through
untranslated---this is the guard that keeps [[nolinemap]] from mangling
messages about files it knows nothing about (or random
\emph{word:number} text that merely looks like a file reference).

<<translator methods>>=
def get_map(self, path):
    """Return the SourceMap for `path`, or None."""
    if path not in self.maps:
        self.maps[path] = self.build_map(path)
    return self.maps[path]

def build_map(self, path):
    """Parse `path` for markers; None if absent, unreadable, bare."""
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
            lines = f.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    markers = []
    for lineno, text in enumerate(lines, start=1):
        for regex in self.marker_regexes:
            match = regex.match(text)
            if match:
                src_file = match.groupdict().get("file") or None
                markers.append((lineno, int(match.group("line")), src_file))
                break
    if not markers:
        return None
    return SourceMap(path, lines, markers)
@ %def get_map build_map

\subsection{Translating columns}

Tangling without [[-L]] preserves lines verbatim except for one
thing: [[nt]] prepends the use site's indentation to every line of an
expanded chunk.  A column in the tangled file is therefore off by
exactly the difference in leading whitespace between the tangled line
and its source line.  Both lines are at hand---the tangled one in the
[[SourceMap]], the source one in the [[.nw]] file---so the shift
costs one subtraction.  When the [[.nw]] file cannot be read (for
example because the compiler ran in another directory), the line
number is still translated and the column is simply left alone; a
slightly-off column is better than no translation at all.  Tabs count
as one column each, which is also how Python and gcc count them.

The [[.nw]] path recorded in a marker is resolved like the compiler
message's own paths: first as given, then relative to the directory
of the tangled file, which covers the common case of a build that
[[cd]]s somewhere else.

<<translator methods>>=
def nw_line(self, path, hint_dir, lineno):
    """Return line `lineno` of .nw file `path`, or None."""
    for candidate in (path, os.path.join(hint_dir, path)):
        if candidate not in self.nw_cache:
            try:
                with open(candidate, encoding="utf-8",
                          errors="replace") as f:
                    self.nw_cache[candidate] = f.read().splitlines()
            except OSError:
                self.nw_cache[candidate] = None
        lines = self.nw_cache[candidate]
        if lines is not None:
            if 1 <= lineno <= len(lines):
                return lines[lineno - 1]
            return None
    return None

def shift_column(self, smap, tangled_line, nw_file, nw_line, column):
    """Shift `column` by the indentation difference of the lines."""
    tangled = smap.line_text(tangled_line)
    source = self.nw_line(nw_file, smap.dir, nw_line) \
        if nw_file else None
    if tangled is None or source is None:
        return column
    def indentation(text):
        return len(text) - len(text.lstrip(" \t"))
    return max(1, column + indentation(source) - indentation(tangled))
@ %def nw_line shift_column

\subsection{Recognizing error messages}

Two shapes of location reference cover most tools.  Python tracebacks
put the location on a line of its own, [[File "f", line n]], possibly
followed by [[, in name]]; that line is matched as a whole and
rebuilt around the translated location.  Nearly everything
else---gcc, clang, shells, linkers, linters---writes
\emph{file:line} or \emph{file:line:column} somewhere in the message,
which is handled by substitution so that any number of references per
line are translated and the rest of the message is preserved
byte for byte.

<<constants>>=
PYTHON_RE = re.compile(r'^(?P<pre>\s*File ")(?P<file>[^"]+)'
                       r'(?P<mid>", line )(?P<line>\d+)(?P<rest>.*)$')
COLON_RE = re.compile(r'(?P<file>[A-Za-z0-9_.+/-]+):(?P<line>\d+)'
                      r'(?::(?P<col>\d+))?')
@ %def PYTHON_RE COLON_RE

<<translator methods>>=
def translate(self, line):
    """Rewrite all translatable references in one message line."""
    match = PYTHON_RE.match(line)
    if match:
        resolved = self.resolve(match.group("file"),
                                int(match.group("line")))
        if resolved is None:
            return line
        nw_file, nw_lineno = resolved
        return (match.group("pre") + nw_file + match.group("mid")
                + str(nw_lineno) + match.group("rest"))
    return COLON_RE.sub(self.colon_reference, line)

def resolve(self, path, lineno):
    """Map `path`:`lineno` to (nw file, nw line), or None."""
    smap = self.get_map(path)
    if smap is None:
        return None
    resolved = smap.resolve(lineno)
    if resolved is None:
        return None
    nw_file, nw_lineno = resolved
    return (nw_file or path, nw_lineno)

def colon_reference(self, match):
    """Translate one file:line[:column] match."""
    path = match.group("file")
    lineno = int(match.group("line"))
    resolved = self.resolve(path, lineno)
    if resolved is None:
        return match.group(0)
    nw_file, nw_lineno = resolved
    result = "%s:%d" % (nw_file, nw_lineno)
    if match.group("col") is not None:
        column = self.shift_column(self.get_map(path), lineno,
                                   nw_file, nw_lineno,
                                   int(match.group("col")))
        result += ":%d" % column
    return result
@ %def translate resolve colon_reference

\subsection{Pumping streams}

One pump copies one stream, translating line by line and flushing
after each line so that output appears as the wrapped command
produces it, not after it exits.  Line endings are preserved as
found.

<<functions>>=
def pump(src, dst, translator):
    """Copy `src` to `dst`, translating each line."""
    for raw in src:
        line = raw.rstrip("\r\n")
        ending = raw[len(line):]
        dst.write(translator.translate(line) + ending)
        dst.flush()
@ %def pump

\subsection{The main program}

Options mirror [[linemark]]'s: [[-L]] gives the exact marker format
that was used when tangling, [[-c]] just the comment leader, and with
neither the default matches [[linemark]]'s default, so the common
case needs no options at all.  Everything after the options is a
command to run; without one, [[nolinemap]] filters standard input,
for use as [[make 2>&1 | nolinemap]].  The [[-h]] output gets an
epilog with worked examples---the two modes are easier to show than
to describe---which needs the raw formatter, since argparse otherwise
reflows the lines into a paragraph.  One trap: argparse
percent-expands option help strings (for [[%(default)s]] and
friends), so the percent signs of the embedded marker format must be
doubled or [[-h]] crashes; the epilog is spared that expansion.

In command mode the child's standard output and standard error are
both translated---compilers split their messages between the two
streams inconsistently---and kept apart, each on its own thread so
that neither stream can fill its pipe and deadlock the child.
[[nolinemap]] exits with the child's exit status, so wrapping a
command in a Makefile does not change what make sees.

<<functions>>=
def main():
    """Run nolinemap; return the exit status."""
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="nolinemap",
        description="Translate messages pointing into tangled files "
                    "so that they point into the .nw sources.",
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        epilog="examples:\n"
               "  notangle -Rfoo.py -filter linemark foo.nw > foo.py\n"
               "  nolinemap python3 foo.py     # run mode, keeps exit"
               " status\n"
               "  make 2>&1 | nolinemap        # pipe mode\n"
               "also installed under the alias noerr")
    parser.add_argument("-L", dest="fmt", metavar="fmt", default=None,
                        help="marker format used when tangling "
                             "(default: %s)"
                             % DEFAULT_FMT.replace("%", "%%"))
    parser.add_argument("-c", dest="leader", metavar="leader",
                        default=None,
                        help="marker comment leader (default: '#')")
    parser.add_argument("command", nargs=argparse.REMAINDER,
                        help="command to run and translate; "
                             "default: translate standard input")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    if args.fmt is not None:
        fmt = args.fmt
    elif args.leader is not None:
        fmt = args.leader + ' %L "%F"'
    else:
        fmt = DEFAULT_FMT
    translator = Translator([fmt_to_regex(fmt)] + BUILTIN_MARKERS)
    command = args.command
    if command and command[0] == "--":
        command = command[1:]
    if not command:
        pump(sys.stdin, sys.stdout, translator)
        return 0
    <<run [[command]] and translate its streams>>
@ %def main

<<run [[command]] and translate its streams>>=
try:
    process = subprocess.Popen(command, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                               stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
except OSError as error:
    sys.stderr.write("nolinemap: %s: %s\n" % (command[0], error))
    return 127
pumps = [
    threading.Thread(target=pump,
                     args=(process.stdout, sys.stdout, translator)),
    threading.Thread(target=pump,
                     args=(process.stderr, sys.stderr, translator)),
]
for thread in pumps:
    thread.start()
for thread in pumps:
    thread.join()
return process.wait()
@


\section{Manual pages}

The manual pages tangle from this same document, so the reference
documentation lives next to the code it describes and travels with it
through every change.  They are committed like the tangled scripts,
and installed only when the [[MAN]] variable is passed to
\emph{make install}---the stock [[contrib/Makefile]] passes only
[[LIB]], so installation of the pages is opt-in.  The alias gets no
page of its own; the Makefile links [[noerr.1]] to [[nolinemap.1]],
mirroring how the main tree links \emph{noweave.1} to
\emph{notangle.1}.

<<linemark.1>>=
.TH LINEMARK 1 "" "noweb contrib"
.SH NAME
linemark \- insert source-location comments into noweb code chunks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B notangle
\fB\-filter\fP '\fBlinemark\fP
[\fB\-L\fP \fIfmt\fP]
[\fB\-c\fP \fIleader\fP]
[\fB\-e\fP]'
\fIfile\fP ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I linemark
is a
.IR noweb (1)
filter that inserts a comment line, by default
.BR "# %L \(dq%F\(dq" ,
wherever the tangled output jumps to a different place in the noweb
source: after the first line of every code chunk (announcing its
second, so that a chunk spliced into the middle of a line never has
its first line broken), before continuation definitions of the same
chunk, and after every line that uses another chunk.
Tangle
.I without
the
.B \-L
option and indentation is preserved, which makes the filter suitable
for indentation-sensitive languages such as Python, for which
.B notangle \-L
produces broken output.
Comment-only lines are exempt from Python's indentation rules, so the
inserted lines are harmless.
Use
.IR nolinemap (1)
to translate tool messages that point into the tangled files back to
the noweb sources.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-L \fIfmt\fP
Set the marker format.
.B %L
expands to the source line number,
.B %F
to the source file name, and
.B %%
to a percent sign; these are the escapes of
.BR "notangle \-L" ,
except that
.B %N
is not supported, because a marker is always exactly one line.
The default is
.BR "# %L \(dq%F\(dq" .
.TP
.B \-c \fIleader\fP
Use
.I leader
as the comment leader in the default format:
.B \-c //
gives
.BR "// %L \(dq%F\(dq" .
When both options are given,
.B \-L
wins.
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-eager
Place every chunk's marker before its first line, as earlier
versions did, and mark after backslash continuations too.
A chunk spliced into the middle of a line then carries the marker
inside the broken line, and a marker after a continuation severs
it, so this suits only consumers that post-process the tangled text
and repair both themselves (vim-noweb's shadow buffers do); in
exchange they get an exact map, with no lines anchored across a
splice.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
notangle -Rfoo.py -filter linemark foo.nw > foo.py
nolinemap python3 foo.py
.fi
.SH BUGS
The first line of a chunk is anchored by counting from the marker
before its use site, so an error on it is reported at the line using
the chunk rather than at its definition.
The same counting covers any leading lines of a chunk that end in a
backslash: a marker there would sever the continuation, so it is
delayed until a line without one, and messages pointing into the
delayed run come out shifted by the length of the splice detour.
A chunk whose first line opens a multi-line string cannot be
protected the same way; the delayed marker lands inside the string.
The very first line of the tangled file has no marker before it at
all; messages pointing there are not translated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR nolinemap (1),
.IR notangle (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Daniel Bosk <dbosk@kth.se>
@

<<nolinemap.1>>=
.TH NOLINEMAP 1 "" "noweb contrib"
.SH NAME
nolinemap \- map messages about tangled files back to noweb sources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nolinemap
[\fB\-L\fP \fIfmt\fP]
[\fB\-c\fP \fIleader\fP]
[\fB\-\-\fP]
[\fIcommand\fP [\fIarg\fP ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I nolinemap
reads the output of a compiler, interpreter, linter, or any other
tool, finds references to files tangled by
.IR notangle (1),
and rewrites them so that they point into the noweb sources, using
the location comments inserted by the
.IR linemark (1)
filter.
It also understands #line directives, so it serves
.B notangle \-L
output as well.
It recognizes Python tracebacks and \fIfile\fP:\fIline\fP[:\fIcolumn\fP]
references; columns are translated too, by comparing the indentation
of the tangled line and its source line.
References to files that do not exist or contain no markers pass
through untouched.
.PP
With a
.IR command ,
nolinemap runs it, translates both its standard output and standard
error, and exits with the command's exit status, so it can wrap
commands in a Makefile transparently.
Without one it translates standard input, for use in a pipe.
.PP
nolinemap is also installed under the alias
.IR noerr ,
after the older Perl program it supersedes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-L \fIfmt\fP
The marker format that was used when tangling; see
.IR linemark (1).
The default is
.BR "# %L \(dq%F\(dq" .
.TP
.B \-c \fIleader\fP
The comment leader that was given to
.B linemark \-c
when tangling.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print a help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
notangle -Rfoo.py -filter linemark foo.nw > foo.py
nolinemap python3 foo.py
make 2>&1 | nolinemap
.fi
.SH BUGS
A hand-written comment that happens to match the marker format
exactly is indistinguishable from a marker and skews the mapping
until the next real marker.
Column translation counts a tab as one column.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR linemark (1),
.IR notangle (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Daniel Bosk <dbosk@kth.se>
@


\section{Testing}

The Makefile's [[test]] target exercises the whole round trip on the
committed fixture [[example.nw]].  The fixture defines a root chunk
that calls a second chunk which raises; the interesting line numbers
are line~13 (the [[raise]]) and line~8 (the call to [[f]]).  Tangling
with the filter,

\begin{verbatim}
notangle -Rexample.py -filter ./linemark example.nw > example.py
\end{verbatim}

yields

\begin{verbatim}
# 5 "example.nw"
def f():
    # 13 "example.nw"
    raise ValueError("expect example.nw line 13")
# 7 "example.nw"

f()
\end{verbatim}

Note that the indentation is intact and that each marker starts a run
of verbatim source lines.  The marker for line~13 is indented because
[[nt]] prefixes every line of an expanded chunk with the use site's
indentation; the marker for line~7 is a line of the outer chunk
itself and stays in column~1.  Python accepts both, since comment-only
lines are exempt from its indentation rules.  Python reports the error at
[[example.py]] lines 7 and~4; the test asserts that

\begin{verbatim}
./nolinemap python3 example.py
\end{verbatim}

reports them as [[example.nw]] lines 8 and~13 instead.  When
[[python3]] is not installed the run is skipped with a note, so the
rest of the contrib tree still builds.


\section{Limitations}

\begin{description}
\item[Mid-expression uses] A chunk used with non-whitespace to its
  left on the line, as in [[x = (<<expr>>)]], gets the used chunk's
  first marker glued into that output line, corrupting the code.
  Statement-position uses---the only idiomatic kind in
  Python---are unaffected.  ([[notangle -L]] shares this class of
  problem: it must break such lines to emit its directive.)
\item[Marker look-alikes] A hand-written comment that happens to
  match the marker format exactly, such as [[# 5 "foo.nw"]], is
  indistinguishable from a marker and will skew the mapping of the
  lines that follow it, until the next real marker.
\item[Line-oriented only] Markers are whole lines, so [[%N]] is not
  supported in formats, and languages without single-line comments
  cannot use the filter.
\item[Tabs] Column translation counts a tab as one column, matching
  Python and gcc.
\end{description}
